      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "For a polynomial of this degree, knowing two of the roots is sufficient to find the polynomial completely if all the roots are distinct. Multiply the given roots by $\\left(\\frac{x}{x-3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+2}\\right)$ to find:\n\\[\\frac{1}{(x-3)} - \\frac{1}{(x-3)^2} - \\frac{2}{(x+2)} + \\frac{4}{(x+2)^2}=0.\\]\nRewriting this equation yields:\n\\[\\frac{1}{x-3} + \\frac{1}{x-3}\\frac{1}{(x+2)} + \\frac{-2}{(x+2)} + \\frac{-2}{(x+2)^2} = 0.\\]\nUsing partial fractions,\n\\[\\frac{1}{(x-3)} + \\frac{-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} - \\frac{2}{(x+2)(x+2)} + \\frac{4}{(x+2)(x+2)} = 0.\\]\nCombine like terms,\n\\[\\frac{7}{(x-3)} - \\frac{5}{(x+2)} = 0.\\]\nObserve that LHS is an expression with valid domain of all $x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\setminus (-\\infty,-2]\\cup [3,+\\infty).$\n\nThus, the solution is $7-5 = \\boxed{2}.$. The answer is 2",
