      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given that two of the roots of the equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) are \\( 3 \\) and \\( -2 \\), we can express the cubic polynomial in its factored form as follows:\n\n\\[ f(x) = a(x-3)(x+2)g(x) \\]\n\nSince the polynomial is a cubic, the other root must be a root of the polynomial that is not part of the linear factors. Let’s denote this third root by \\( r \\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[ f(x) = a(x-3)(x+2)(x-r) \\]\n\nExpanding this, we get:\n\n\\[ f(x) = a(x^3 - (3 + 2 - r)x^2 + (3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot r - 3) x - 6r) \\]\n\\[ f(x) = a(x^3 - (5-r)x^2 + (6+r) x - 6r) \\]\n\nThus, comparing the coefficients with the original polynomial \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\), we have:\n\n\\[ b = -a(5-r) \\]\n\\[ c = a(6+r) \\]\n\\[ d = -6ar \\]\n\nWe are asked to find the value of \\( \\frac{b+c}{a} \\). Substituting the expressions for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\):\n\n\\[ \\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-a(5-r) + a(6+r)}{a} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-a(5-r) + a(6+r)}{a} = \\frac{-a \\cdot 5 + a \\cdot r + a \\cdot 6 + a \\cdot r}{a} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-5a + ar + 6a + ar}{a} = \\frac{a(6 + 2r)}{a} = 6 + 2r \\]\n\nSo, the value of \\( \\frac{b+c}{a} \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\]\n\nIf you have any further questions, feel free to ask!",
